<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    MSVC `__real@` FP-constant COMDAT recovery (`option msvcfpconst on|off`,
    default on).

    MSVC never encodes a floating-point literal into the instruction stream (x87
    and SSE both load one from memory), so it emits each literal as a COMDAT
    whose NAME spells the value. COMDAT folding keeps the definition in exactly
    one translation unit, so in every other object that symbol is UNDEFINED: no
    section, no bytes, and the loader binds it to a synthetic extern slot with
    nothing behind it. The defined half fares no better at the shipped defaults:
    its bytes are mapped and read-only, but folding a read-only global is gated
    by the program-wide `option readonly`, which is default-off. Either way the
    expression comes out written in opaque addresses.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. Both passes clear it
    and load the real i386 COFF fixture, which carries all four spellings in both
    states: `__real@3fb999999999999a` (16 hex, IEEE double bits) and
    `__real@3f000000` (8 hex, IEEE float bits) DEFINED, plus
    `__real@8@3ffec90fdaa22168c000` and `__real@4@3fff8000000000000000` (the
    VC6-era x87 80-bit form, at both storage widths) UNDEFINED.

    Pass 1 (`option msvcfpconst off`) reproduces the defect: both operands of
    each expression are `dat_<addr>`, the defined half and the undefined half
    alike.

    Pass 2 turns it back on and is the shipped default. All four constants render
    as literals: pi/4 and 0.1 in `_scale`, 1.0 and 0.5 in `_fscale`.

    The gate is LOADER-tier, so both `option` lines have to run BEFORE their
    `load file`; they set the process env var the loader reads, and an option
    applied after the load would arrive long after the bytes were snapshotted.
    That is also why pass 2 names the option explicitly instead of relying on the
    default: pass 1 left the env var set to `off` in this same process. The
    option's own default state is covered by the Rust gate
    `decompiler/crates/kuna-console/tests/verify_msvcfpconst.rs`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option msvcfpconst off</com>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/msvcfpconst_i386.obj</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function _scale</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>load function _fscale</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option msvcfpconst on</com>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/msvcfpconst_i386.obj</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function _scale</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>load function _fscale</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFPCONST #1: off, both operands of _scale are opaque -- the undefined x87 COMDAT and the defined IEEE-double one alike" min="1" max="1">\(float10\)a0 \* \(float10\)dat_402000 \+ \(float10\)dat_400038;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFPCONST #2: off, both operands of _fscale are opaque too" min="1" max="1">\(float10\)dat_402010 \* \(float10\)a0 \* \(float10\)dat_400040;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFPCONST #3: on, _scale reads pi/4 from the undefined 20-hex x87 name and 0.1 from the defined 16-hex IEEE double" min="1" max="1">\(float10\)a0 \* \(float10\)0\.7853981633974483 \+ \(float10\)0\.1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFPCONST #4: on, _fscale reads 1.0 from the 20-hex x87 name at float width and 0.5 from the defined 8-hex IEEE float, the modern MSVC spelling" min="1" max="1">\(float10\)1\.0 \* \(float10\)a0 \* \(float10\)0\.5;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFPCONST #5: no opaque operand survives the on pass -- every dat_ line belongs to the off one" min="2" max="2">dat_4</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFPCONST #6: the recovery changes the operands only -- both passes agree on the signature" min="2" max="2">float10 _scale\(float8 a0\)</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
